{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nThe advisory `sweep (windows)` job fails 84 tests in tests/unit/test_review_bot.py (scheduled run 41230936309, ref 0886e96f03a; run 40797257532 shows the same 84). 81 fail with `RuntimeError: Network access is forbidden in unit/scripts tests`, 2 with `State object has no attribute 'config'`, and 1 (`test_sigkill_during_a_store_write_orphans_the_mkdir_lock`) with `module 'signal' has no attribute 'SIGKILL'`. The default `_current_revision` stub from eastmost-uncheered-nematode (688e564ff91) did not change this.\n\nRoot cause (evidence in the first comment): on Windows, `socket.socketpair()` is the stdlib's `socket._fallback_socketpair`, which connects an AF_INET loopback pair inside the process. Every new asyncio event loop builds its self-pipe that way (`ProactorEventLoop._make_self_pipe`). The autouse network guard in tests/conftest.py (`_guard_socket_connect`) refuses every AF_INET connect, so `asyncio.run`, and any TestClient lifespan, fails before test code runs. A macOS simulation that installs the fallback as `socket.socketpair` reproduces 83 of the 84 failures exactly; the remaining one is the POSIX-only SIGKILL test. Commit 5697f2e1117 already set the rule that local IPC (AF_UNIX) passes the guard because it never touches the network; the in-process loopback pair is the Windows form of that IPC.\n\n## Approach\n1. In tests/conftest.py, let `_guard_socket_connect` pass an AF_INET/AF_INET6 connect only when its direct caller is the stdlib socketpair fallback, identified by the caller frame's code object being `socket._fallback_socketpair.__code__` (on Python 3.11 the Windows fallback is `socket.socketpair` itself, so its code object is included too; on POSIX `socket.socketpair` never calls connect, so including it allows nothing new). Every other AF_INET/AF_INET6 connect, including a loopback connect from test or product code, stays blocked. No new fixture, marker, environment variable, or patch is added; the guard's docstring states the exemption and the reason.\n2. In tests/unit/test_review_bot.py, mark `test_sigkill_during_a_store_write_orphans_the_mkdir_lock` with `pytest.mark.skipif(os.name != \"posix\", reason=...)`, the precedent used in tests/unit/test_bridge_runner_spawn.py, with a reason naming SIGKILL and the fcntl lock leg as POSIX-only. The test body is unchanged.\n3. Leave `test_lifespan_cancel_await_is_bounded_for_a_task_slow_to_cancel` (rewritten in 782aa2921f6) unchanged; it fails on Windows only through the same event-loop path.\n\nRejected: switching tests to `WindowsSelectorEventLoopPolicy` (the selector loop also builds its self-pipe with `socket.socketpair()`, so it hits the same guard); allowing every loopback connect (widens the guard to any local server, which 5697f2e1117 did not intend).\n\n## Scope\n- tests/conftest.py: the caller-frame exemption in `_guard_socket_connect` and its docstring.\n- tests/unit/test_network_guard_local_ipc.py: regression tests.\n- tests/unit/test_review_bot.py: the POSIX-only skip on the SIGKILL test.\nNo production code under src/ changes.\n\n## Testing\nRegression tests in tests/unit/test_network_guard_local_ipc.py, run under the autouse guard on every platform:\n- `socket._fallback_socketpair()` returns a connected pair that carries bytes both ways. Fails before the fix with the guard's RuntimeError.\n- With `socket.socketpair` monkeypatched to `socket._fallback_socketpair` (the Windows configuration), `asyncio.run` of a trivial coroutine completes. Fails before the fix at `_make_self_pipe`.\n- Teeth: a plain AF_INET connect from test code to a listening 127.0.0.1 socket in the same process still raises the guard's RuntimeError, so the exemption is not a loopback allowance. The existing TEST-NET-3 test keeps covering a remote address.\nAlso run tests/unit/test_review_bot.py and the review-bot sibling modules with and without the scratch Windows-simulation plugin, then `make lint` and `make typecheck`.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] The point where the RuntimeError escapes on Windows is identified and recorded on this ticket.\n- [ ] The 3 regression tests above fail on the code before the fix and pass after it.\n- [ ] With the scratch plugin that installs `socket._fallback_socketpair` as `socket.socketpair`, tests/unit/test_review_bot.py has 0 failures on macOS (it had 83 before the fix), and without the plugin it still has 0 failures.\n- [ ] `make lint` and `make typecheck` exit 0.\n- [ ] A scheduled `sweep (windows)` run after the fix shows no failure in tests/unit/test_review_bot.py. [non-codebase]\n  provenance: the GitHub Actions run id and its result, recorded on this ticket."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1793500247905861001,"uuid":"52d479f8-7863-48bf-9071-4b061dc55d82"}